WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.493
Three identical balls.

2
00:00:01.493 --> 00:00:03.054
Three different tracks.

3
00:00:03.054 --> 00:00:05.158
Each ball gets the same launch.

4
00:00:05.158 --> 00:00:09.094
Tile, short grass, or thick
carpet: which ball will travel

5
00:00:09.094 --> 00:00:10.790
furthest before it stops?

6
00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:13.912
Make your prediction,
then watch for evidence.

7
00:00:13.912 --> 00:00:15.499
All three begin still.

8
00:00:15.499 --> 00:00:19.610
The same launcher setting
sends each ball across a smooth

9
00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:20.548
launch strip.

10
00:00:20.548 --> 00:00:24.876
A timing gate confirms the same
entry speed before each test

11
00:00:24.876 --> 00:00:25.453
surface.

12
00:00:25.453 --> 00:00:27.328
Now the launcher is clear.

13
00:00:27.328 --> 00:00:30.285
Tile gives the longest
stopping distance.

14
00:00:30.285 --> 00:00:31.512
Grass is shorter.

15
00:00:31.512 --> 00:00:32.882
Carpet is shortest.

16
00:00:32.882 --> 00:00:35.270
Motion can tell several stories.

17
00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.360
Still to moving means start.

18
00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.793
More distance in the
same time means speed up.

19
00:00:40.793 --> 00:00:43.927
Less distance each
moment means slow down.

20
00:00:43.927 --> 00:00:45.943
No new distance means stop.

21
00:00:45.943 --> 00:00:48.480
A new path means
change direction.

22
00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.290
A physicist compares
before and after.

23
00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:56.311
Before the launch, the
carpet ball is still.

24
00:00:56.311 --> 00:00:58.577
Just after contact,
it is moving.

25
00:00:58.577 --> 00:01:00.706
Later, its gaps become smaller.

26
00:01:00.706 --> 00:01:03.453
Finally, its position
no longer changes.

27
00:01:03.453 --> 00:01:05.994
That sequence is
measurable evidence.

28
00:01:05.994 --> 00:01:08.149
Look closely at the contact.

29
00:01:08.149 --> 00:01:12.614
While the launcher touches the
ball, launcher on ball is a

30
00:01:12.614 --> 00:01:13.615
forward push.

31
00:01:13.615 --> 00:01:17.694
Once the launcher separates,
that contact force ends.

32
00:01:17.694 --> 00:01:22.159
The ball can still be moving
even though the kick is over.

33
00:01:22.159 --> 00:01:25.580
The moving ball still interacts
with its surroundings.

34
00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.418
The surface touches the ball.

35
00:01:27.418 --> 00:01:31.282
In this carpet model, friction
and carpet deformation produce

36
00:01:31.282 --> 00:01:33.183
a backward surface resistance.

37
00:01:33.183 --> 00:01:37.048
The greater slowing effect gives
a shorter stopping distance.

38
00:01:39.248 --> 00:01:42.764
To compare surfaces fairly,
keep the ball, entry speed, and

39
00:01:42.764 --> 00:01:43.955
start line the same.

40
00:01:43.955 --> 00:01:45.385
Change only the surface.

41
00:01:45.385 --> 00:01:48.424
Measure from the surface
start to the resting ball.

42
00:01:48.424 --> 00:01:52.059
Repeat each run, because one
surprising run may not represent

43
00:01:52.059 --> 00:01:52.774
the pattern.

44
00:01:52.774 --> 00:01:55.019
Check all three runs
before the averages.

45
00:01:55.019 --> 00:01:58.414
Tile measures three hundred and
thirty-five, three hundred and

46
00:01:58.414 --> 00:02:01.699
forty-five, and three hundred
and forty centimetres: average

47
00:02:01.699 --> 00:02:03.013
three hundred and forty.

48
00:02:03.013 --> 00:02:05.094
Grass averages two
hundred and twenty.

49
00:02:05.094 --> 00:02:07.065
Carpet averages one
hundred and ten.

50
00:02:07.065 --> 00:02:09.420
The graph and tracks
show the same pattern.

51
00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:12.883
A tempting explanation says
the kick force rides inside the

52
00:02:12.883 --> 00:02:14.350
ball and slowly runs out.

53
00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:17.637
But the contact arrow ended
when the launcher separated.

54
00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:20.924
The remaining evidence shows
a ball moving while surface

55
00:02:20.924 --> 00:02:22.919
friction acts
against that motion.

56
00:02:22.919 --> 00:02:24.680
The kick is not stored inside.

57
00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.254
A force can change
direction as well as speed.

58
00:02:30.254 --> 00:02:33.042
A rolling ball meets
an angled bumper.

59
00:02:33.042 --> 00:02:37.517
The bumper-on-ball force points
away from the bumper surface.

60
00:02:37.517 --> 00:02:42.065
That force changes the ball's
motion into a new path: straight

61
00:02:42.065 --> 00:02:43.312
in, diagonal out.

62
00:02:43.312 --> 00:02:47.072
Suppose we replace thick
carpet with a more deformable,

63
00:02:47.072 --> 00:02:51.174
higher-resistance rubber mat
while everything else stays the

64
00:02:51.174 --> 00:02:51.515
same.

65
00:02:51.515 --> 00:02:53.566
Predict the stopping distance.

66
00:02:53.566 --> 00:02:57.463
Would it be longer than tile,
between tile and carpet, or

67
00:02:57.463 --> 00:02:58.830
shorter than carpet?

68
00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:00.744
Use the model, then test it.

69
00:03:00.744 --> 00:03:03.957
Our testable prediction
is shorter than carpet.

70
00:03:03.957 --> 00:03:05.590
Use this motion routine.

71
00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:07.292
Compare before and after.

72
00:03:07.292 --> 00:03:08.857
Name the motion change.

73
00:03:08.857 --> 00:03:10.830
Name the interacting objects.

74
00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.756
Then connect the force
to visible evidence.

75
00:03:13.756 --> 00:03:16.410
The ball does not
carry a used-up kick.

76
00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:20.085
Motion changes because objects
are interacting now, or

77
00:03:20.085 --> 00:03:23.760
interacted earlier while
other forces continue to act.
